<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="fo4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>FO4: The Laplace Transform (First-Order Equations)</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use the Laplace transform to solve first-order, linear differential equations with or without forcing terms.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    
    <subsection xml:id="laplace-transform-intro">
        <title>The Laplace Transform</title>
        
        <definition xml:id="def-laplace-transform">
            <title>The Laplace Transform</title>
            <idx><h>Laplace transform</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For a function <m>f(t)</m> which satisfies the condition <m>|f(t)| \leq Me^{ct}</m> for some constants <m>M,c</m>. Then, we define the <term>Laplace transform</term> of <m>f(t)</m> to be
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{L}\lrbrace{f(t)} = F(s) = \int_0^\infty f(t)e^{-st}\, dt
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-inverse-laplace-transform">
            <title>Inverse Laplace Transform</title>
            <idx><h>Laplace transform (inverse)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>inverse Laplace transform</term> of a function <m>F(s)</m> is the function <m>\inv{\mathcal{L}}\lrbrace{F(s)} = f(t)</m> whose Laplace transform is <m>F(s)</m>, i.e the inverse Laplace transform <q>undoes</q> the Laplace transform.                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The Laplace transform of a constant function <m>f(x) = k</m> is computed below:
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \mathcal{L}\lrbrace{k} \amp = \int_0^\infty ke^{-st}\, dt </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = k\cdot\lim_{N\to\infty} \int_0^N e^{-st}\, dt </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = k\cdot\lim_{N\to\infty} -\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st}\bigg\rvert_0^N</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = k\cdot\lim_{N\to\infty} \lrpar{-\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-sN} + \dfrac{1}{s}e^{-s\cdot 0}}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = k\cdot \dfrac{1}{s} </mrow>
                    </md>
                    So <m>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{k} = \dfrac{k}{s}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The inverse Laplace transform of the function <m>F(s) = \dfrac{10}{s^2 + 100}</m> is <m>f(t) = \sin (10t)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that for <m>f(t) = t</m>, <m>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{t} = \dfrac{1}{s^2}</m>. You will need to use integration by parts:
                    <md>
                        \int a\, db = ab - \int b\, da
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that for <m>f(t) = e^{at}</m> (where <m>a</m> is constant), 
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{L}\lrbrace{e^{at}} = \dfrac{1}{s-a}
                    </md>
                    What condition(s) on <m>s</m> must be considered in order for the Laplace transform to exist?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <table xml:id="table-laplace-transforms-first">
            <title>Table of Laplace Transforms (First)</title>
            <tabular halign="center" top="medium">
                <row bottom="medium">
                    <cell><m>f(t) = {\mathcal L}^{-1}(F(s))</m></cell><cell><m>F(s) = {\mathcal L}(f(t))</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>1</cell><cell><m>1/s</m>, <m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>e^{at}</m></cell><cell><m>1/(s - a)</m>, <m>s \gt a</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>t^n</m>, <m>n \in {\mathbb N}</m></cell><cell><m>n!/s^{n+1}</m>, <m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>\sin at</m></cell><cell><m>a/(s^2 + a^2)</m>, <m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>\cos at</m></cell><cell><m>s/(s^2 + a^2)</m>, <m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>\sinh at</m></cell><cell><m>a/(s^2 - a^2)</m>, <m>s \gt |a|</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>\cosh at</m></cell><cell><m>s/(s^2 - a^2)</m>, <m>s \gt |a|</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>e^{at} \sin bt</m></cell><cell><m>b/[(s-a)^2 + b^2]</m>, <m>s \gt a</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>e^{at} \cos bt</m></cell><cell><m>(s-a)/[(s-a)^2 + b^2]</m>, <m>s \gt a</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="medium">
                    <cell><m>t^n e^{at}</m>, <m>n \in {\mathbb N}</m></cell><cell><m>n!/(s - a)^{n + 1}</m>, <m>s \gt a</m> </cell>
                </row>
                
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="laplace-transform-properties">
        <title>Properties of the Laplace Transform</title>
        
        <theorem xml:id="thm-laplace-transform-linear">
            <title>The Laplace Transform Is Linear</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f,g</m> be two functions with Laplace transforms <m>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{f(t)} = F(s)</m> and <m>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{g(t)} = G(s)</m>, respectively. Let <m>\alpha,\beta\in\R</m> be constants. Then,
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{L}\lrbrace{\alpha f(t) + \beta g(t)} = \alpha \mathcal{L}\lrbrace{f(t)} + \beta\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{g(t)} = \alpha F(s) + \beta G(s)
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
        
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Convince yourself why this is true- think about the definition of the transform.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="laplace-transform-solving-first-order">
        <title>Solving First Order Equations with the Laplace Transform</title>
        
        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We can compute the Laplace transform of a derivative. Consider a function <m>f(t)</m> and its derivative, <m>f'(t)</m>. We can use integration by parts to find <m>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{f'(t)}</m>:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{f'(t)} \amp = \lim_{N\to\infty} \int_0^N f'(t)e^{-st}\, dt </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{N\to\infty}\left[f(t)e^{-st}\bigg\rvert_0^N - \int_0^N -s\cdot f(t)e^{-st}\right]</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -f(0) + s\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{f(t)}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = sF(s) - f(0)</mrow>
                    </md>
                    So, taking the Laplace transform of a derivative requires the knowledge of the initial value of the function <m>f(t)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f'(t) = 2t</m> and <m>f(0) = 5</m>; to find <m>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{f'(t)}</m>, we know that
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{f'(t)} \amp = sF(s) - f(0)</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = sF(s) - 5 </mrow>
                    </md>
                    But we also know that <m>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{2t} = \dfrac{2}{s^2}</m> from <xref ref="table-laplace-transforms-first"/>, so we have
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{2}{s^2} = sF(s) - 5
                    </md>
                    or 
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{2}{s^2} + 5 = sF(s)
                    </md>
                    so that
                    <md>
                        F(s) = \dfrac{2}{s^3} + \dfrac{5}{s}
                    </md>
                    Using <xref ref="table-laplace-transforms-first"/> to find the inverse transform, we get 
                    <md>
                        f(t) = t^2 + 5
                    </md>
                    which is what we would have gotten if we solved the initial value problem using the techniques we already know.      
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <assemblage>
            <title>Solving First-Order ODEs with the Laplace Transform</title>
            <p>
                To solve a differential equation of the form <m>ay'(t) + by(t) = f(t)</m>,
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Compute the transform of the left hand side
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the transform of the right hand side
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Solve for <m>F(s)</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Use algebra to rewrite the right hand side so that you can apply the inverse transform from <xref ref="table-laplace-transforms-first"/>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose we wish to solve the initial value problem
                    <md>
                        2y' - y = \cos(2x),\quad y(0) = -1
                    </md>
                    using the Laplace transform.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Begin by taking the transform of both sides. 
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{2y' - y} \amp = 2\mathcal{L}\lrbrace{y'} - \mathcal{L}\lrbrace{y} </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 2(sY(s) - y(0)) - Y(s) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 2sY(s) + 2 - Y(s) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = (2s-1)Y(s) + 2</mrow>
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{L}\lrbrace{\cos(2x)} = \dfrac{s}{s^2 + 4}
                    </md>
                    So all together, the transformed equation is
                    <md>
                        (2s-1)Y(s) + 2 = \dfrac{s}{s^2 + 4}
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Rearranging to solve for <m>Y(s)</m>, we have
                    <md>
                        Y(s) = \dfrac{s}{(s^2+4)(2s-1)} - \dfrac{2}{2s-1}
                    </md>
                    The first term requires partial fraction decomposition. Write it as
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{s}{(s^2+4)(2s-1)} = \dfrac{As+B}{s^2+4} + \dfrac{C}{2s-1}
                    </md>
                    Now, 
                    <md>
                        s = (As+B)(2s-1) + C(s^2+4)
                    </md>
                    from which we get the system
                    <md>
                        \begin{cases}
                            2A + C \amp = 0 \\
                            2B - A \amp = 1 \\
                            4C - B \amp = 0
                        \end{cases}
                    </md>
                    and the solutions of <m>A = -\dfrac{1}{17}</m>, <m>B = \dfrac{8}{17}</m> and <m>C = \dfrac{2}{17}</m>. This gives 
                    <md>
                        Y(s) = \dfrac{(-1/17)s + (8/17)}{s^2 + 4} + \dfrac{2/17}{2s-1} - \dfrac{2}{2s-1} = \dfrac{(-1/17)s+(8/17)}{s^2+4} + \dfrac{-32/17}{2s-1}
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    To use the inverse transform, we need to write each term on the right hand side in a way which is consistent with <xref ref="table-laplace-transforms-first"/>. First, 
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \dfrac{(-1/17)s+(8/17)}{s^2+4} - \dfrac{32/17}{2s-1}\amp = \dfrac{(-1/17)s}{s^2+4} + \dfrac{8/17}{s^2+4} - \dfrac{32/17}{2s-1}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -\dfrac{1}{17}\lrpar{\dfrac{s}{s^2+4}} + \dfrac{4}{17}\lrpar{\dfrac{2}{s^2+4}} - \dfrac{16}{17}\lrpar{\dfrac{1}{s-1/2}} </mrow>
                    </md>
                    Now applying the inverse transform to each term gives us
                    <md>
                        y(x) = -\dfrac{1}{17}\cos(2x) + \dfrac{4}{17}\sin(2x) - \dfrac{16}{17}e^{1/2x}
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
                <p>
                    We could have used the integrating factor instead of the Laplace transform; this would be a good place to practice that technique.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that the solution to the IVP
                    <md>
                        y' + 6y = e^{-t},\quad y(0) = 6
                    </md>
                    is given by
                    <md>
                        y(t) = \dfrac{1}{5}e^{-t} + \dfrac{29}{5}e^{-6t}
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </subsection>
</section>